# Prefix words and encoding requests in front of an instruction, one statement
# a group, each held to GNU as 2.47.
#
# * In any order: GNU as reads `{rex2}`, `{evex}`, `{nooptimize}` and the
#   prefix words in one loop. A prefix word behind a request was "no such
#   instruction" (`{rex2} lock addl $1, (%rax)`, `{nooptimize} rep movsb`).
# * A prefix word that is the whole statement is its byte: `lock` and the
#   `rep`s were, the others were refused (`rex64`, which GCC writes on the
#   line in front of the `call` of a TLS sequence, `data16`, `cs`).
# * `lock` in front of an EVEX encoding is refused: the APX forms of the
#   lockable instructions are not lockable (lccc wrote `f0 62 ..`, an
#   undefined opcode).
# * `data16` in front of a REX2 prefix is the 0x66 in front of it (it was
#   "same type of prefix used twice").
# * `jmpabs` takes no size and no address override (they were written).
# * A segment word and a string instruction's own segment are one prefix.
# * A jump to a label behind a `cs`, `ds` or REX word is relaxed with the
#   word in front (`cs jmp 1f; 1:` is 2e eb 00; it kept its near form).
#
# Not here, because lccc does not follow GNU as: a prefix word GNU as leaves
# out of a direct branch with a warning ("skipping prefixes on `call'": any
# segment word on `call`, `fs`/`gs`/`addr32` on `jmp` and `jcc`), which lccc
# writes, as Clang does; `data16` and the REX words in front of an APX EVEX
# encoding, which GNU as folds into the prefix and lccc refuses. The
# encoder's unit tests (`prefix_word_tests`) pin those.
;;; xchg_rax_rax
.text
xchg %rax, %rax
;;; xchg_eax_eax
.text
xchg %eax, %eax
;;; xchg_ax_ax
.text
xchg %ax, %ax
;;; rex2_xchg_rax_rax
.text
{rex2} xchg %rax, %rax
;;; rex2_xchg_ax_ax
.text
{rex2} xchg %ax, %ax
;;; rex2_xchg_rax_rcx
.text
{rex2} xchg %rax, %rcx
